# AGENTS.md

Your name is Dave and developers will use your name when interacting with you.

The manual has been split into separate chapter files in the `.manual/` directory. Read only the chapters relevant to your task. Each chapter file begins with its own section index — open the chapter file when you need section-level detail.

You will want to look at `Chapter 20: Developer Guide` for detailed information about the project structure, code, and workflows.

## Basic Rules

- After modifying any `.go` file, always run `go vet` and `gofmt -s -w` on the changed files.
- After modifying any `.go` file, always run `staticcheck` and `go fix` on the changed package.
- You need this env var to run tests
  - export GITHUB_WORKSPACE=<Root Location Of Kronk Project>
- Never perform a full repo test run and never run tests from this location `sdk/kronk/tests`.
- Download model fixtures with `kronk model pull <SOURCE> --local` (and `--proj` when needed), not direct downloads into scratch directories. Keep them in Kronk's normal model store so developers can reuse them after verification.

## Documentation Rules

- Reserve `BREAKING_CHANGES.md` for incompatible changes to Kronk's existing APIs or observable behavior, including wire formats, configuration, and defaults; describe the incompatibility and required migration. Document compatible additive features and opt-in options in the manual instead.
- Upgrading Kronk requires upgrading its bindings and matching native libraries together. A mandatory dependency or native-library upgrade, including an upstream ABI change, is not a breaking change by itself when Kronk's public APIs and observable behavior remain compatible. Keep matching-version and installation instructions in README or the manual, not `BREAKING_CHANGES.md`.
- When updating README's compatibility table, use `sdk/kronk/kronk.go:Version` without the `-rc` suffix for the first row (for example, `1.33.3-rc` becomes `1.33.3`). Update that version's row in place while it is a release candidate; do not add a separate `main` or `unreleased` row or treat the existing row as a completed release.
- Focus user-facing documentation on Kronk's semantics and specification: what features mean, their observable behavior, supported inputs and outputs, defaults, constraints, and limitations. Do not explain internal code structure, private functions, or implementation mechanics.
- Be concrete for CLI usage, installation, and configuration. Provide actionable steps, exact commands and flags, file locations, configuration examples, and expected results so users can perform the task without reading the source code.
- Verify documented behavior against the code, but describe the public contract rather than how the code implements it. Keep implementation details in developer-focused documentation, such as Chapter 20: Developer Guide, when they are needed for contributors.

## Skills

Project skills live in `.agents/skills/`. Load the matching skill before the work it covers.

| Skill                                                                | Load when                                                                                                                                                                                    |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| [`use-modern-go`](.agents/skills/use-modern-go/SKILL.md)             | Writing, editing, or reviewing any `.go` file: modern syntax for the Go version in `go.mod`.                                                                                                 |
| [`kronk-service-architecture`](.agents/skills/kronk-service-architecture/SKILL.md) | Writing, editing, or auditing Go under `cmd/`, `sdk/`, or `examples/`: ownership and import direction, HTTP request/response types, type-set enums, SDK errors and `errs.FromSDK`, lifecycle and inference invariants, branching style. |
| [`kronk-pr-review`](.agents/skills/kronk-pr-review/SKILL.md)         | Any review request ("review this PR", "review my changes", "pre-commit review", one aspect such as tests or security). Claude Code; read-only; reviews only the change unless a full review is asked for; runs CI's Go static checks, plus focused race tests for a maintainer's PR only; writes reports to `.reviews/`. |

## Config Tool (`kronk config`)

`cmd/kronk/config` asks questions to write `model_config.yaml`. Keep it in step when these change:

- **Model settings** (`models.ModelConfig` in `sdk/tools/models/kronkconfig.go`, or enum values in `sdk/kronk/model/config_types.go`): the questions are hand-written in `Chat()` in `cmd/kronk/config/questions/chat.go`. Add, rename or remove the question there. `TestQuestionKeys` fails on a renamed key or removed value, but not on a new setting with no question.
- **Server settings** (`config` struct in `cmd/server/api/services/kronk/config.go`): questions are generated from the struct. Give every new field a `conf:"help:..."` tag (no commas in the text); `TestSettings` fails without it. A new top-level group needs a label in `sections` in `cmd/kronk/config/questions/server.go`.
- **GPU backends** (a new `gpu_*` device type): add it to `GPUTypes` in `cmd/kronk/config/source/machine.go`.
- **Audio and image models** (`BuckyModelConfig`, `MalinaModelConfig` in `sdk/tools/modelconfig`): questions are in `Audio()` and `Image()` in `cmd/kronk/config/questions/media.go`; `TestMediaQuestionKeys` checks them.

After a change, run `go test ./cmd/kronk/config/... ./cmd/server/api/services/kronk/`.

## MCP Services

Kronk has an MCP service and these are settings:

```
"mcp": {
    "Kronk": {
        "type": "remote",
        "url": "http://localhost:9000/mcp",
        "type": "streamableHttp",
        "apis": [
            {
                "api": "web_search",
                "desc": "Performs a web search for the given query. Returns a list of relevant web pages with titles, URLs, and descriptions. Use this for general information gathering, research, and finding specific web resources."
            }
        ],
    }
}
```

